{
  "paraId": "3DAAC3F5",
  "style": "",
  "text": "In a retrospective study of 428 critically-ill patients with severe sepsis or septic shock, pulmonary and abdominal infections predominated, in keeping with our findings [25]. The initial infection escaped identification in nearly 20% of our patients, as reported previously [28]. An increase over time in GNB infections among neutropenic patients with cancer and sepsis has been reported [28]. GNB infections predominated in our cohort but did not increase significantly between the early and recent periods. Multidrug-resistant pathogens contributed fewer than 5% of the organisms recovered in our patients compared to nearly 25% in a 2010–2017 study from Spain confined to patients with hematological malignancies [29], reflecting differences in epidemiology between the two countries. Comparable temporal trends were reported by Legrand et al. in 428 ICU patients with cancer, neutropenia, and severe sepsis or septic shock, in whom a microbiological diagnosis was established in 55.5% and fever of unknown origin accounted for 11.9%, with a hospital mortality of 49.8% overall that decreased from 58.7% in 1998–2003 to 43.0% in 2004–2008 [25]."
}

